  "live_records": {
    "q-48243": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48243",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14262965/assets/69d48bb02c5c0e7552b4ff9dd5fb7db1c91422f5b60d864f3ac7ca60f194b56f.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.",
      "worked_solution": "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=2,$ so we need to exclude this point. \n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(2,\\infty )$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- The intervals $(0,\\infty ),$  $(-2,4),$  $(-\\infty ,6),$  and $(1,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $x=2,$ so the function is not continuous on any of these  these intervals.",
