    "q-76092": {
      "dom_id": "step-q76092",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,1)?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown.",
      "worked_solution": "The correct answer is the following. \n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14262965/assets/065781240f1f839195486b0b2914c25c44639095d36b8e36eb7273014adbdf51.png)\n\n   \n\nIndeed, we have that $p(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nNotice that: \n\n- $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-\\infty ,1),$\n\n- $h(x)$ and $f(x)$ are not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-\\infty ,1),$\n\n- $q(x)$is not continuous at $x=-1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-\\infty ,1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
