    "q-104891": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104891",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14262965/assets/69fd9e95c75086b7cd4d027c13ff2bbbdf0e543579115cc1151e1e3febbcd732.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?",
      "worked_solution": "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-2,2].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $[-2,2]$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-1,2]$ \n\nNotice that\n\n- $[-3,-1]$ and $[-10,0]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-2,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $[0,5]$ and $[1,3]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=2,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
