    "q-105415": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105415",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14262965/assets/c48ce844a63e4e5fcc7db98cb8e571548d7fe44194d914f2e74193cdf71d83d7.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]$\n\nII. $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-4,4)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and also left continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-4,4)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
